WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.420
Field techniques: controlled crosses, good seed and fair trials.

2
00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:10.510
This is the video for module 2 of Agricultural Hybridization and Crop Improvement.

3
00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:17.650
Breeding is controlled pollination. You choose the mother and the father, and block all other pollen.

4
00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:22.360
To keep a variety pure, you keep outside pollen away.

5
00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:28.020
You will check seed is genuine and alive, and handle treated seed safely.

6
00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:31.360
And you will run a trial you can trust.

7
00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:38.370
Step 1. Cover the ear shoot with a shoot bag as soon as it appears, before any silk comes out.

8
00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:45.040
Step 2. When silks emerge, cut them straight across in the afternoon, and re-bag at once.

9
00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:52.070
Step 3. The same afternoon, cover the father's tassel with a tassel bag and staple it.

10
00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:59.980
Step 4. Next morning, after the dew dries, bend the tassel and tap the bag so pollen falls inside.

11
00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:07.760
Step 5. Lift the shoot bag quickly, pour the pollen over the silks, and cover the ear with the tassel bag.

12
00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:13.620
Step 6. Write the cross on the bag and a tag, mother first, with the date.

13
00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:19.850
For tomato, remove the anthers from a bud before it opens. That is called emasculation.

14
00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:30.730
For example, Funke grows 0.5 ha of white OPV maize in Oyo State. She needs 10 kg of seed, and saves 15 kg.

15
00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:33.900
She avoids the outer four rows.

16
00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:40.480
Before harvest, in the field, she ribbons upright, disease-free plants with good husk cover.

17
00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:49.320
She marks at least 200 plants. The common mistake is choosing from too few plants, or picking big cobs at the barn.

18
00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:53.390
She uses grain from the middle of each good cob.

19
00:01:54.640 --> 00:02:07.670
In Nigeria, the National Agricultural Seeds Council, NASC, registers seed companies and certifies seed. Check the label for the variety, lot number, test date, producer and any treatment.

20
00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:19.060
Be careful with loose seed, missing lot numbers, very low prices or re-stitched bags. Keep the receipt and the empty bag, and report fake seed to NASC.

21
00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.660
Before planting, count out 100 seeds at random.

22
00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:28.210
Lay them on damp tissue or cloth, and roll it loosely.

23
00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:32.920
Keep it moist and warm, for about 7 days for maize.

24
00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:43.390
Count the seeds with a healthy root and shoot. For example, Ifeoma in Anambra counted 86 out of 100. That is 86%.

25
00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:50.250
So for 1 hectare she needs about 18.6 kg, and keeps 20 kg.

26
00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:57.790
If germination is low, many advisers suggest buying fresh seed rather than simply sowing more.

27
00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:08.320
Seed coated with a coloured powder or liquid has usually been dressed with a pesticide. Never eat it, sell it as grain, or feed it to animals.

28
00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:15.180
Wear gloves when handling it, do not touch your face, and wash your hands with soap after planting.

29
00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:20.970
Lock the bags away from children and livestock. Never reuse them for food.

30
00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:28.920
GM varieties must be approved by NBMA, the National Biosafety Management Agency, before release.

31
00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:38.300
A single comparison can be fooled by a wet corner. Step 1. Choose your usual variety, the check, plus one or two new ones.

32
00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:44.990
Step 2. Make three blocks across any slope. Each block holds every variety once.

33
00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:50.480
Step 3. In each block, draw folded papers to decide the order.

34
00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:59.500
Step 4. Plant 4 rows of 5 m. The outer rows are borders. Harvest only the 2 middle rows, the net plot.

35
00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:06.890
Step 5. Record plant stand, days to 50% silking, disease scores and lodging.

36
00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:11.900
Manage every plot the same, so only the variety differs.

37
00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:23.600
For example, Rukayat in Kaduna harvests a net plot of 7.5 m2. Variety A gives 4.2 kg of grain at 20% moisture.

38
00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:31.010
Wetter grain weighs more, so she corrects to 15% moisture: 3.95 kg.

39
00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:37.530
Scaled up to a hectare, that is about 5,267 kg.

40
00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:47.660
Across three blocks, Variety A averages 5,056 kg per hectare, and her usual seed 4,133.

41
00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:58.250
Variety A wins by 923 kg per hectare, and it won in every block. Winning in every block matters as much as the average.

42
00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:11.150
To remember. Bag the ear early. Select from many plants in the field. Test germination. Treat dressed seed as poison. And replicate, randomise and correct moisture.

43
00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:17.800
Now make three controlled crosses, run a germination test, and plan and peg your trial.

44
00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:20.090
Continue with the lessons below.
